// Where QUIC stops deriving its own secrets and starts using TLS's — one sentence of specification,
// and three things the types cannot say about it.
//
// `packages/quic/src/keys.wac` describes itself exactly:
//
// > The join between the two specifications is one sentence: QUIC takes TLS's traffic secret and
// > runs `HKDF-Expand-Label` on it with `quic key`, `quic iv` and `quic hp` instead of TLS's own
// > `key` and `iv`. … This file is the sentence.
//
// So it is the smallest seam in the tree between two packages implementing two specifications, and
// the failure it warns about is the quietest:
//
// > A transcript that included the framing would produce keys that are perfectly well-formed and
// > that no peer shares, which decrypts as noise and **looks exactly like a wrong Diffie-Hellman**.

import { Bytes } from "core";
import { Digest32 } from "@/packages/crypto/src/digest.wac";

/**
 * The hash of every handshake message so far — and the type that would have prevented the warning.
 *
 * `@/packages/crypto/src/digest.wac` gives a `Digest32` whose guarantee is *32 bytes because a hash
 * produced it*. That is not enough here. The hazard is not a wrong **length**, it is a hash of the
 * **wrong bytes** — the shipped signature is `handshakeKeys(u8[] dhe, u8[] transcript, bool
 * isServer)`, and every wrong argument to `transcript` is 32 bytes and is a real digest of
 * something.
 *
 * What would say it is a digest that knows what it digested: `Digest32<Transcript>`, a phantom
 * parameter carrying provenance rather than shape. Then `transcriptOf(messages)` is the only way to
 * make one, and hashing the frames instead of the messages produces a `Digest32<Frames>` that this
 * function refuses.
 *
 * **Which is the same request `@/packages/ssz` made about roots** — *"a `Chunk` from a `BeaconState`
 * and one from a `SyncCommittee` compare `false` rather than failing to type-check"* — and it was
 * declined there because `sha256` cannot know a `T`. It cannot here either. The difference is that
 * `ssz`'s consequence is a comparison that answers `false`, and this one's is a connection that
 * silently cannot be decrypted by anybody, indistinguishable from a failed key exchange.
 *
 * So the same missing feature has two prices, and the second is the argument.
 */
export const struct Transcript {
  Digest32 hash;

  /** Every handshake message with its four-byte header, and nothing else. The only constructor. */
  Transcript of(Bytes messages) { … }

  /** Two runs of messages, concatenated then hashed — not two hashes combined. */
  Transcript ofTwo(Bytes first, Bytes second) { … }
}

/** Which side's traffic secret. `bool isServer` in the shipped signature. */
export enum Side { Client, Server }

/** The packet protection keys for one direction. */
export struct PacketKeys { Bytes key; Bytes iv; Bytes headerProtection; }

/**
 * Handshake keys — the first real ones, since they depend on the exchange and on every handshake
 * byte both sides have seen.
 *
 * `dhe` is `secret` and the answer is not, which is the hole `@/packages/crypto/src/secret.wac`
 * names and this file makes concrete across a **package boundary**: the shared secret arrives from
 * `@/packages/tls`'s key schedule, is `secret` there, and arrives here as an ordinary `Bytes`
 * because a return position cannot carry the qualifier.
 */
export PacketKeys handshakeKeys(secret Bytes dhe, const Transcript t, Side side) { … }

/** The same for application keys, after the handshake completes. */
export PacketKeys applicationKeys(secret Bytes dhe, const Transcript t, Side side) { … }

// ## 1. A `secret` that crosses a package boundary loses the qualifier at the seam
//
// `@/packages/tls/src/keyschedule.wac` is this directory's `secret` consumer — *"eleven functions
// that hand a secret to each other, and that is the thing nothing had tried"* — and its finding was
// that `secret` has no return position, so each of the eleven re-declares it on the way in.
//
// This file is the same absence one layer out. The traffic secret is derived in `tls`, is `secret`
// there, crosses to `quic` as a return value, and arrives as a plain `Bytes` that this file marks
// `secret` again on its own parameters. **Eleven functions inside one package is a nuisance; a
// package boundary is where the re-declaration is a different author.**
//
// Worth stating because it changes what the qualifier would have to be. Inside one file, a checker
// could plausibly infer the taint. Across a package, the only thing that carries it is the
// *signature*, and a qualifier with no return position cannot appear in one.
//
// ## 2. `bool isServer` decides which of two secrets, and the shipped comment says it is a hazard
//
// > `isServer` picks which side's traffic secret, and it is …
//
// A `bool` selecting between two derivations, where getting it backwards produces keys that are
// well-formed and shared with nobody — the same silent failure as the transcript. `Side` costs
// nothing and this directory has now found the shape in `@/packages/codec`'s `pad`,
// `@/packages/git`'s `isDir`, and here; the difference is that the first two are wrong in a way a
// test sees.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **The two specifications' boundary is a comment.** *"Everything above that line is
// `packages/tls`'s and everything below it is packet protection that `initial.wac` already does."*
// That sentence is the whole architecture of the seam and there is no way to state it — no module
// boundary, no type, no import that says *this is where RFC 9001 takes over from RFC 8446*. A
// package boundary is the nearest thing and it is one directory away from where the line actually
// falls.
//
// **`PacketKeys` is three `Bytes` and they are 16, 12 and 16.** Eighth package to want a length in a
// type, and the first where all three fields are different lengths of the same type in one struct —
// so a constructor that transposed `key` and `headerProtection` builds a value that is wrong and
// well-formed, and both are 16 bytes.
//
// **Nothing says these keys are for one direction.** `handshakeKeys(…, Side.Client)` and the server
// pair are the same type, so a receiver that protects with the wrong set gets, again, packets nobody
// can read. `Side` fixes the *argument* and not the *result*, which would need the phantom parameter
// §1 wanted for the transcript — so one missing feature would answer both, and it is the one `ssz`
// asked for and was told `sha256` cannot know a `T`.
